<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ngoenyang</h1><p class="st-meta">638 CE – 1262 CE (approx.) · capital Chiang Saen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The early Tai principality of Chiang Saen in the Golden Triangle, the homeland of King Mangrai, who built it into the kingdom of Lan Na.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Northern Thai chronicles trace Ngoenyang, also called Hiran Ngoenyang, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lao-chakkaraj">Lao Chakkaraj</a>, who is said to have founded a dynasty on the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> in the 7th century, with its seat later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-saen">Chiang Saen</a>. These early dates rest on legend; the principality is better known from the 13th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ngoenyang was one of several Tai muang, small valley principalities based on irrigated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and ruled by hereditary chiefs with kin ties to neighbouring rulers. Its lords were linked by marriage to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sipsong-panna">Chiang Hung</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xishuangbanna">Sipsongpanna</a> and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> was spreading in the region but the earliest history is mostly legendary.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a> became ruler around 1259 and expanded southward, founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-rai">Chiang Rai</a> in 1262 and conquering <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hariphunchai">Hariphunchai</a> in 1292. He moved his capital to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-chiang-mai">Chiang Mai</a> in 1296, and Ngoenyang became the core of the kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-saen">Chiang Saen</a> remained an important northern city.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ngoenyang.jpg" alt="Ngoenyang" width="320" height="359"><figcaption>Image: Thomson Walt · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ngoenyang_1156-1180.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>638 CE – 1262 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chiang Saen</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Lao Chakkaraj, Mangrai</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Theravada Buddhism, Tai spirit worship</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Northern Thai</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hiran Ngoenyang, Chiang Saen kingdom, Yonok</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=950&amp;focus=ngoenyang" aria-label="Ngoenyang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7377 1427 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ngoenyang lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7697 1683L7697 1683"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7697 1683h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7697 1683h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7697 1683h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chiang Saen. <a href="/atlas?year=950&amp;focus=ngoenyang">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
